<div class="side-by-side">
  <h2>The Challenge</h2>

  <p>
    Devsisters, the studio behind the CookieRun franchise, and PRESS.A, its
    internal studio, were launching a creator tournament for CookieRun:
    OvenSmash. It would feature 24 of the most recognized creators in mobile
    gaming, each streaming the event to their own audience. That reach came with
    a production problem: the broadcast had to work for traditional 16:9 viewers
    and for 9:16 vertical-first platforms at the same time.
  </p>
</div>

<div class="side-by-side">
  <p>
    <b>More than a resize.</b> Every graphic, animation and visual element had
    to work in both formats and stay clear and consistent.
  </p>
  <p>
    <b>Double the workload.</b> Without a unified system, every asset would be
    built and managed twice, adding creative work and live production
    complexity.
  </p>
  <p>
    <b>Two broadcasts, one team.</b> Running and synchronizing two separate live
    shows in real time would have multiplied the risk on show day.
  </p>
</div>

<h2>The Solution</h2>

<div class="side-by-side">
  <p class="big">One system, two formats</p>
  <p>
    We built the visual system around a central focus area, the safe region for
    all priority content. Every graphic was designed in 16:9 around that shared
    structure, with secondary elements extending past it, so each scene could be
    recomposed for 9:16 with only minor layout adjustments. On the production
    side, the broadcast workflow was automated to generate both formats
    simultaneously, with no second show to manage or keep in sync.
  </p>
</div>

